9
00:40:49,285 --> 00:40:55,285
Caifás, played by Mattia Sbragia.

10
00:45:21,428 --> 00:45:27,428
The Passion of the Christ is a 2004 American epic biblical drama film co-produced and
directed by Mel Gibson from a screenplay he wrote with Benedict Fitzgerald.

11
00:49:53,571 --> 00:49:59,571
It stars Jim Caviezel as Jesus, Maia Morgenstern as his mother
Mary, and Monica Bellucci as Mary Magdalene.

12
00:54:25,714 --> 00:54:31,714
It depicts the arrest, trial and crucifixion of Jesus, largely according to the canonical gospels as well as additional
accounts such as the purported mystical visions by Anne Catherine Emmerich and the Friday of Sorrows.

13
00:58:57,857 --> 00:59:03,857
The film primarily covers the final twelve hours before
Jesus Christ's death, known as "the Passion".

14
01:03:30,000 --> 01:03:36,000
It begins with the Agony in the Garden of Olives (i.e., Gethsemane), continues with the betrayal of Judas Iscariot, the Flagellation of Christ, the
suffering of Mary as prophesied by Simeon, the crucifixion and death of Jesus, and ends with a brief depiction of his resurrection.

15
01:08:02,142 --> 01:08:08,142
The narrative is interspersed with moments in Jesus's life, such as the Last
Supper and the Sermon on the Mount, and moments of Jesus' early life.

16
01:12:34,285 --> 01:12:40,285
The film was mostly shot in Italy.

17
01:17:06,428 --> 01:17:12,428
The dialogue is entirely in reconstructed Aramaic, Hebrew, and Latin.

18
01:21:38,571 --> 01:21:44,571
Although Gibson was initially against it, the film is subtitled.

19
01:26:10,714 --> 01:26:16,714
The Passion of the Christ was released by Newmarket Films on February 25, 2004.

20
01:30:42,857 --> 01:30:48,857
The film was controversial and received polarized reviews; some critics called the film a religious and holy experience, praising the
performances, production values, and John Debney's score, while some found it antisemitic and the violence extreme and draining.

21
01:35:15,000 --> 01:35:21,000
The film grossed $612.1 million worldwide against a $30 million budget, and became the fifth
highest-grossing film of 2004 internationally at the end of its theatrical run.

22
01:39:47,142 --> 01:39:53,142
It is the highest-grossing (inflation unadjusted) Christian film of all time,
as well as the highest-grossing independent film of all time.

23
01:44:19,285 --> 01:44:25,285
It was the highest-grossing R-rated film in the US, at $370.8
million, a record which remained unbroken for 20 years.

24
01:48:51,428 --> 01:48:57,428
It received three nominations at the 77th Academy Awards in 2005, for
Best Makeup, Best Cinematography, and Best Original Score.

25
01:53:23,571 --> 01:53:29,571
Two sequels, The Resurrection of the Christ: Part One and Part Two,
are due to be released in 2027, with a new cast.

26
01:57:55,714 --> 01:58:01,714
The film explores themes of mission, suffering,
roman empire, christianity, jewry.

27
02:02:27,857 --> 02:02:33,857
The tone is uplifting and dark and thought_provoking.
